.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH git-veil-removeperson 1  "git-veil-removeperson " 
.SH NAME
git\-veil\-removeperson \- Remove a collaborator from the keyring and re\-sign it
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgit\-veil removeperson\fR [\fB\-\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-key\-store\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-stdin\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIEMAIL\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Remove a collaborator from the keyring and re\-sign it
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR \fI<REMOTE>\fR [default: origin]
Git remote name
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-store\fR \fI<KEY_STORE>\fR
Key store directory (default: $HOME/.git\-veil)
.TP
\fB\-\-passphrase\-stdin\fR
Read the passphrase for a passphrase\-protected private key from stdin (exactly one line). Wins over the GITVEIL_PASSPHRASE environment variable; never pass a passphrase as a CLI argument
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fIEMAIL\fR>
Email of the collaborator to remove
.SH EXTRA
Removes a collaborator\*(Aqs entry from the keyring and re\-signs it with the
owner\*(Aqs private key. The existing keyring signature is verified against
the pinned trusted key before any modification. After removal the
ex\-collaborator can no longer decrypt newly hidden files — but files
hidden while they were a member were encrypted to their key, so rotate
the underlying secrets if that matters.

Requires init, an established trust pin (trust), and the owner\*(Aqs private
key in the local key store (import).

EXAMPLES
  $ git\-veil removeperson alice@example.com
  $ git\-veil removeperson alice@example.com \-\-passphrase\-stdin < pass.txt
